---

## Retrieval Quality Pipeline (symmetric across both stores)

```
Stage 1 — Recall (parallel)
├── Dense search:  cosine similarity on nomic-embed-text-v2 vectors
└── Sparse search: BM42 lexical match

Stage 2 — Fusion
└── RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion) — merge ranked lists, top-K candidates

Stage 3 — Re-rank
└── BGE-reranker-v2-m3 — cross-encoder per-pair relevance score

Stage 4 — Final scoring (differs per store)
├── Conversation: composite α·rerank + β·importance_decayed + γ·recency + δ·usage
└── Document:    rerank_score + payload filters (doc_type, language, date range)
```

Both stores must store `dense + sparse` vectors. Diagrams 02 and 03 reflect this.

---

## Conversation Importance Scoring

At write time (Diagram 03), a small LLM scores each turn on:
- emotional disclosure
- stated preference
- explicit commitment
- domain fact
- correction of prior turn

Output: `importance: float[0,1]`. Decayed daily by `importance · e^(-λ·days)`, λ=0.01.

Boosted on retrieval (`retrieved_count` and `last_retrieved_at` updated every hit).
Manually boosted via `boost_importance` MCP tool when user explicitly references a past turn.

---

## Document Ingestion — Two-Track Model

### Track A — Always runs (retrieval indexing)
```
Chandra OCR → Enrich (heading injection, table-to-text, topic tags, xref)
            → Chunk (parent-child) → Dedup → Dual embed → Write Qdrant + Postgres
```
No gate. Document is searchable seconds after upload.

### Track B — Conditional (knowledge extraction)
```
If doc_class has ontology mapping:
  Extract entities → Validate swarm (3 agents) → Gate → graph_write
Else:
  Skip Track B, log only
```
Validation gate:
- `conf ≥ 0.85` + all 3 agents agree → `graph_write` MCP
- `0.6 ≤ conf < 0.85` or disagreement → `quarantine_write` → human review
- contradicts high-conf existing fact → `audit_log(REJECTED)`

---

## Graph-Search Cypher Generation Flags

Three independent env-gated levers control the FalkorDB graph-search path. Effective only when `ENABLE_GRAPH_SEARCH=True`.

| Setting | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `ENABLE_GRAPH_SEARCH` | `False` | Master switch for the whole graph-search path. When off, the orchestrator skips the Cypher LLM call entirely and relies on hybrid vector retrieval only. |
| `ENABLE_SCHEMA_AWARE_CYPHER` | `True` | When on, the Cypher-gen system prompt is augmented with the live FalkorDB schema (node labels, relationship types, properties per label) plus schema-anchored few-shot examples. Falls back to the static prompt if introspection fails. |
| `CYPHER_SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS` | `300` | Schema introspection runs at most once per TTL window per process. |
| `ENABLE_CYPHER_SELF_HEALING` | `False` | When on, an invalid Cypher generation is re-prompted to the same LLM with the validator's rejection reason, up to `CYPHER_SELF_HEAL_MAX_ATTEMPTS` times. Each retry is another LLM round-trip — flip on when you have a bigger model and want max graph recall. |
| `CYPHER_SELF_HEAL_MAX_ATTEMPTS` | `3` | Hard cap on Cypher generation attempts when self-healing is on. Per Neo4j's iterative-refinement research, most correction lands on attempt 2; 3 is the standard ceiling. |

Recommended profiles:
- **Small model (≤ 1B params, e.g. `qwen3.5:0.8b`):** `ENABLE_GRAPH_SEARCH=False`. The model can rarely produce valid Cypher; the extra LLM call is wasted compute.
- **Medium model (~7B, e.g. `qwen2.5:7b`):** `ENABLE_GRAPH_SEARCH=True`, `ENABLE_SCHEMA_AWARE_CYPHER=True`, `ENABLE_CYPHER_SELF_HEALING=False`. Schema injection alone is usually enough.
- **Large model (≥ 14B, GPU-backed):** Add `ENABLE_CYPHER_SELF_HEALING=True`. Latency cost is acceptable; recall improves on hard questions.

Validation in [src/docai/orchestrator/engine.py](src/docai/orchestrator/engine.py) (`_normalise_generated_cypher`) is always read-only enforcement — write tokens (`CREATE`, `MERGE`, `DELETE`, `SET`, `REMOVE`, `DROP`, `DETACH`) are rejected regardless of which flags are on.

---

## MCP Tool Boundary

Tools in the registry (Diagram 06) only access DOCUMENT stores and FalkorDB:

| Tool group | Tools | Access |
|---|---|---|
| Retrieval | `semantic_search`, `get_parent_chunk`, `cross_ref_lookup` | doc Qdrant, Postgres |
| Graph | `graph_query`, `entity_link`, `check_contradiction` | FalkorDB |
| Validation | `verify_fact`, `ontology_check` | doc chunks, ontology |
| Importance | `boost_importance`, `get_importance_history` | episodic Qdrant (write only, gated) |
| Write | `graph_write`, `quarantine_write`, `audit_log` | FalkorDB, Postgres |

`semantic_search` for conversation does NOT exist as an MCP tool. Conversation retrieval is orchestrator-direct.

---

## Deployment Topology (Diagram 04)

| Node | Components | Resource |
|---|---|---|
| Edge | Nginx + TLS | CPU |
| Application | FastAPI, async orchestrator, Celery workers | CPU |
| MCP + Ruflo | Ruflo MCP server, MCP registry, doc ingestion agent swarm | CPU |
| GPU | vLLM tiers 1/3, embedding service, **Chandra OCR 2** | GPU (A100/H100) |
| Data | Redis, Qdrant, FalkorDB, PostgreSQL | CPU + storage |
| Observability | Prometheus, Grafana, Langfuse | CPU |

